Small changes, derivatives, and first-order approximations (@ql-small-changes: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @quantitative-literacy, #source @ql-source-street, #source @ql-source-insight, #source @ql-source-uncertainty, #quantitative-literacy "Sensitivity-and-conditioning.lingua#r_43NVV3KJX9W9R026T1NKCYMP3T", #metrology "../../metrology/uncertainty/Sensitivity-and-correlation.lingua#r_0EF35BB106S2F2ATJBXBR49DFR", #electromagnetism "../../../physics/electromagnetism/mathematics/Gradient-divergence-curl-and-integral-theorems.lingua#r_05M47N16QDCC5B927TEYXAMMA4", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Calculus-fields-differential-equations-and-solving-a-thermal-model.lingua#r_5BYXE3KKKGPFBR1BGCVY7ZABNG", #fluid-dynamics "../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/mathematics/Coordinates-fields-and-local-versus-material-change.lingua#r_3ZSP7RX2565ZAA91ZD418BAEVN", #mechanical-engineering "../../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/dynamics/Differentiate-a-position-and-check-the-physical-units.lingua#r_049JX2TZBB3WS6CDR6574772SG", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/particles/Relaxation-time-and-the-distance-before-stopping.lingua#r_69TCZNTFWEQS1C3QEFDW0R5RJ1") { r_4T51QH8VRCNNMY5WD30VJS5P52
**Read:** Street-Fighting Mathematics, Taking out the big part, and The Art of Insight's treatment of proportional reasoning and easy cases. Follow the algebra in their derivations. NIST TN 1297 appendix A shows why this language enters measurement calculations.

For y = f(x), the finite change is f(x + h) − f(x). Dividing by h gives an average rate over that interval, with units of output per input. The derivative f′(x) is the limit of this ratio as h approaches zero when the limit exists. A first-order approximation writes f(x + h) ≈ f(x) + f′(x)h for a sufficiently small h. Small must be checked against the function and the required accuracy.

For f(x) = x², the finite change is 2xh + h², so f′(x) = 2x and the neglected term is exactly h². At x = 10 and h = 0.1 in consistent numerical units, the exact change is 2.01 while the first-order prediction is 2. The difference is visible and its scale can be justified. Near x = 0 the relative approximation behaves differently, even though the formula is the same.

For several inputs, a partial derivative changes one input while holding the others fixed. In V = πd²h/4, the sensitivity to d is πdh/2 and the sensitivity to h is πd²/4. Expanding the finite changes verifies these expressions without assuming a previous calculus course. A simultaneous first-order change sums the individual sensitivities times their input changes; products and higher powers of small changes are omitted.

**Work:** derive the diameter and height sensitivities from finite differences and check their units. Compare the approximation with direct evaluation for 1%, 5%, and 20% input changes. State a tolerance on the approximation and find a range satisfying it. For f(x) = |x| at zero, show that approaching from different signs gives different slopes, so the same differentiable-model treatment does not apply there.

This is a working introduction to local change, not a proof of all differentiation rules. Retain the derivation and domain checks; the sensitivity and uncertainty readings immediately use them, and later calculus can reuse this preparation.

